QB.add(14,[{"id":14001,"t":"H&E Staining","f":"ma06","s":"Why does haematoxylin stain the nucleus purple?","o":["It is a basic dye binding acidic structures such as DNA","It is an acidic dye binding basic structures such as DNA","It is a basic dye binding basic structures such as proteins","It is an acidic dye binding cytoplasmic proteins and collagen","It is a lipid stain binding the nuclear membrane"],"e":"<b>Haematoxylin</b> is a <b>basic</b> dye, so it stains <b>acidic (basophilic)</b> structures purple – DNA (nucleus, nucleolus), RNA and carbohydrates such as cartilage molecules. <b>Eosin</b> is <b>acidic</b> and stains <b>basic (acidophilic)</b> structures pink – cytoplasmic proteins and extracellular fibres. Opposites attract.","w":["Haematoxylin is basic.","DNA is acidic.","Eosin binds protein.","Not a lipid stain."],"k":"Haematoxylin (basic) → acidic structures purple.","rn":[["","Haematoxylin","Haematoxylin","Basic dye; Stains acidic (basophilic) structures; Purple"],["","Eosin","Eosin","Acidic dye; Stains basic (acidophilic) structures; Pink"]]},{"id":14002,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"An H&amp;E section of fibrous tissue is mostly pink with a few purple dots. What do these represent?","o":["Pink = collagen of the matrix; purple dots = fibroblast nuclei","Pink = fibroblast cytoplasm; purple dots = collagen bundles","Pink = collagen of the matrix; purple dots = goblet cells","Pink = mucus; purple dots = fibroblast nuclei","Pink = red cells only; purple dots = platelets"],"e":"In fibrous tissue the purple dots are the <b>fibroblasts</b> (nuclei), which produce the pink <b>collagen fibres</b> (protein) – few cells, mostly <b>non-cellular extracellular matrix</b>.","w":["Collagen is pink.","Fibroblasts.","Mucus is clear.","Not."],"k":"Fibrous tissue: pink collagen, purple fibroblast nuclei.","rn":[["","On fibrous tissue H&E, what are the purple dots?","On fibrous tissue H&E, what are the purple dots?","Fibroblasts"],["","On fibrous tissue H&E, what is all the pink?","On fibrous tissue H&E, what is all the pink?","Collagen fibres (protein); Extracellular matrix"],["","What do the few fibroblasts do in fibrous connective tissue?","What do the few fibroblasts do in fibrous connective tissue?","Make collagen fibres; Replenish the extracellular matrix"]]},{"id":14003,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"A tracheal biopsy shows tall cells whose nuclei lie at different heights, yet every cell touches the basement membrane. Name this epithelium.","e":"Pseudostratified (ciliated) columnar epithelium: a single layer that looks layered because tall columnar cells have nuclei at different heights; every cell contacts the basement membrane. Found in the trachea, with goblet cells.","k":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar.","img":{"src":"14/pseudo","cap":"Airway lining, H&amp;E."},"a":["pseudostratified ciliated columnar","pseudostratified columnar","pseudostratified","pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","pseudostratified columnar epithelium","respiratory epithelium"],"rn":[["","What is the subtype of pseudostratified epithelium?","What is the subtype of pseudostratified epithelium?","Ciliated"],["","Why is pseudostratified only columnar?","Why is pseudostratified only columnar?","Columnar cells are tall; And rectangular"],["","Which cells form pseudostratified epithelium?","Which cells form pseudostratified epithelium?","Columnar cells"]]},{"id":14004,"t":"Types of Human Tissue","s":"Which are the four tissue types that make up all organs and systems?","o":["Epithelial, connective, muscle and nervous","Epithelial, connective, muscle and glandular","Epithelial, bone, muscle and nervous","Connective, glandular, vascular and nervous","Squamous, cuboidal, columnar and transitional"],"e":"All human body systems and organs consist of <b>four tissue types</b>: <b>epithelial, connective, muscle, nervous</b> (“Every Cell Matters Now”).","w":["Glands are epithelium.","Bone is connective.","Not.","Epithelial shapes."],"k":"Four tissue types.","rn":[["","What are the 4 types of human tissue?","What are the 4 types of human tissue?","Epithelial; Connective; Muscle; Nervous"]]},{"id":14005,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","f":"ma08","s":"Which pairing of epithelial cell shape, location and function is correct?","o":["Cuboidal – glands, kidney tubules – secretion, absorption","Cuboidal – vessels, lungs – diffusion, filtration","Squamous – glands, kidney tubules – secretion, absorption","Columnar – vessels, lungs – diffusion, filtration","Squamous – GI tract – absorption, protection"],"e":"<b>Squamous</b> (flat): heart, lungs, vessels (endothelium), membranes (mesothelium) – diffusion, filtration, secretion. <b>Cuboidal</b>: glands, kidney tubules – secretion, absorption. <b>Columnar</b>: GI tract, uterine tubes – absorption, secretion.","w":["Squamous.","Cuboidal.","Squamous.","Columnar."],"k":"Epithelial shapes: location and function.","rn":[["","Where is cuboidal epithelium found?","Where is cuboidal epithelium found?","Glands; Kidney tubules"],["","What is the function of cuboidal epithelium?","What is the function of cuboidal epithelium?","Secretion; Absorption"],["","Which epithelium: kidney tubules?","Which epithelium: kidney tubules?","Simple cuboidal"]]},{"id":14006,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"A student writes that “blood vessels are lined by squamous epithelial cells.” What is the more precise description?","o":["Endothelial cells: squamous-like, named for function and origin","They are mesothelial cells: squamous-like membrane cells lining vessels","They are squamous cells: endothelium is just another name for them","They are cuboidal cells specialised for diffusion","They are columnar cells with microvilli"],"e":"<b>Endothelium</b> is a subtype: vessels are lined by cells structured like squamous epithelial cells, but because of different <b>function and origin</b> they are <b>endothelial cells</b>. It is incorrect to call them squamous epithelial cells.","w":["Mesothelium lines membranes.","Different origin/function.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Vessels: endothelium, not “squamous epithelium”.","rn":[["","Are blood vessels made of squamous epithelial cells?","Are blood vessels made of squamous epithelial cells?","No: endothelial cells; A subtype of squamous"]]},{"id":14007,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Why are cuboidal and columnar cells described as “a bit more functional” than squamous cells?","o":["Larger cytoplasm allows secretion and absorption","Larger nuclei allow faster division and turnover","More layers allow greater protection","Keratin allows waterproofing","Cilia allow movement of mucus"],"e":"Cuboidal and columnar cells have <b>larger cytoplasm</b>, so more added functions (secretion/absorption). Squamous cells are flat, mainly <b>barrier</b> and filtration.","w":["Not stated.","Not always layered.","Skin.","Not all."],"k":"Larger cytoplasm → more function.","rn":[["","Why are cuboidal cells more functional than squamous cells?","Why are cuboidal cells more functional than squamous cells?","Larger cytoplasm; More added functions"],["","What is the function of cuboidal epithelium?","What is the function of cuboidal epithelium?","Secretion; Absorption"],["","What is the function of columnar epithelium?","What is the function of columnar epithelium?","Absorption; Secretion"]]},{"id":14008,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"An epithelium has a single layer of cells. What is it called, and which shapes can it include?","o":["Simple; squamous, cuboidal or columnar","Simple; squamous or cuboidal only","Stratified; squamous, cuboidal or columnar","Pseudostratified; columnar only","Transitional; variable shapes"],"e":"A single layer of squamous, cuboidal or columnar cells = <b>simple</b> epithelium.","w":["Columnar too.","Multiple layers.","Appears layered.","Variable layers."],"k":"Simple = single layer.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of epithelial cell shape?","What are the 3 types of epithelial cell shape?","Squamous; Cuboidal; Columnar"]]},{"id":14009,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","f":"ma11","s":"Which pairing of stratified squamous epithelium type and location is correct?","o":["Keratinised – skin; non-keratinised – mouth","Keratinised – mouth; non-keratinised – skin","Keratinised – skin; non-keratinised – bladder","Keratinised – trachea; non-keratinised – mouth","Keratinised – vessels; non-keratinised – skin"],"e":"<b>Stratified</b> squamous epithelium: <b>keratinised (K)</b> in the <b>skin</b>, <b>non-keratinised (NK)</b> in the <b>mouth</b>; function: protection against injury.","w":["Swapped.","Transitional.","Pseudostratified.","Endothelium."],"k":"K = skin; NK = mouth.","rn":[["","Where is non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Where is non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Mouth"]]},{"id":14010,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"What does the keratin layer contribute to skin epithelium?","o":["It makes the tissue impervious and waterproof","It makes the tissue elastic for stretching","It increases surface area for absorption","It propels mucus along the surface","It secretes protective hormones"],"e":"The <b>keratin layer</b>, produced by <b>keratinocytes</b>, makes the tissue <b>impervious and waterproof</b>.","w":["Transitional.","Microvilli.","Cilia.","Not."],"k":"Keratin waterproofs skin.","rn":[["","Keratinised","Keratinised","Includes a keratin layer; Produced by keratinocytes; Makes tissue impervious and waterproof"]]},{"id":14011,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","f":"ma12","s":"In the trachea, columnar cells appear to form several layers because their nuclei lie at different heights. What confirms it is actually a single layer?","o":["Every cell contacts the basement membrane","Every cell reaches the apical surface","The cells are flat with central nuclei","The cells are keratinised at the surface","The number of layers changes with stretch"],"e":"<b>Pseudostratified</b> epithelium: columnar cells, some squashed between others so nuclei are not in a line, but <b>every cell touches the basement membrane</b> – still a single layer. It is <b>ciliated</b> in the trachea, propelling mucus.","w":["Not all reach the surface.","Columnar.","Not.","Transitional."],"k":"Pseudostratified: all touch basement membrane.","rn":[["","What proves pseudostratified is one layer?","What proves pseudostratified is one layer?","All cells touch basement membrane"]]},{"id":14012,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Why is the false appearance of layers a feature of columnar cells only?","o":["Tall, rectangular cells let nuclei sit at different heights","They are flat, so nuclei overlap in layers","They lack a basement membrane, so the cells stack on each other","They are keratinised, so they look layered","They are multinucleated, so nuclei scatter"],"e":"The false appearance of layers is a feature of <b>columnar cells only</b>, because they are <b>tall and rectangular</b>.","w":["Tall.","Every cell touches the basement membrane.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Pseudostratified = columnar only.","rn":[["","Why is pseudostratified only columnar?","Why is pseudostratified only columnar?","Columnar cells are tall; And rectangular"],["","Columnar","Columnar","Rectangular; Tall"]]},{"id":14013,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","f":"ma13","s":"Which epithelium has a variable number of layers allowing stretching, and where is it found?","o":["Transitional – bladder and urethra","Transitional – trachea and bronchi","Pseudostratified – bladder and urethra","Stratified keratinised – skin","Simple columnar – GI tract"],"e":"<b>Transitional</b> epithelium has a <b>variable number of layers</b> – elastic, allowing the lining to <b>stretch</b> – found in the urinary system (<b>bladder, urethra</b>).","w":["Urinary.","Trachea.","Not stretch.","Not."],"k":"Transitional = bladder, stretch.","rn":[["","Where is transitional epithelium found?","Where is transitional epithelium found?","Urinary system; Bladder; Urethra"]]},{"id":14014,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Which statement correctly compares cilia and microvilli?","o":["Cilia: motile, microtubule core; microvilli: microfilament core, absorption","Cilia: motile, microfilament core; microvilli: microtubule core, absorption","Cilia: absorption, microtubule core; microvilli: motile, microfilament core","Cilia and microvilli: both motile with microtubule cores","Cilia and microvilli: both increase absorption in the gut"],"e":"<b>Cilia</b>: long, fine, <b>motile</b> projections with a <b>microtubule</b> core that <b>beat synchronously</b> (upper respiratory tract). <b>Microvilli</b>: finger-like, <b>microfilament</b> core, <b>increase apical surface for absorption</b> (gut).","w":["Cores swapped.","Functions swapped.","Microvilli not motile.","Cilia move mucus."],"k":"Cilia: move (microtubules); microvilli: absorb (microfilaments).","rn":[["","What is the core of microvilli made of?","What is the core of microvilli made of?","Microfilaments"]]},{"id":14015,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Why can epithelial tissues such as the epidermis and small intestine renew quickly?","o":["High turnover from tissue-specific stem cells","High turnover driven by circulating blood stem cells","Low turnover, as their cells are post-mitotic","High turnover driven by fibroblasts in the matrix","No turnover; damaged cells are never replaced"],"e":"Epithelium has <b>high cell turnover</b>; self-renewal depends on <b>tissue-specific stem or progenitor cells</b>, e.g. <b>intestinal stem cells (Lgr5)</b>, hair follicle cells, lung epithelium.","w":["Tissue-specific.","High.","Fibroblasts are connective tissue.","They renew."],"k":"Epithelial renewal via stem/progenitor cells.","rn":[["","What does epithelial self-renewal depend on?","What does epithelial self-renewal depend on?","Tissue-specific stem/progenitor cells"],["","What is epithelial tissue characterised by?","What is epithelial tissue characterised by?","High cell turnover; Leading to tissue renewal"]]},{"id":14016,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Name the protein marker of intestinal stem cells.","e":"Intestinal stem cells are characterised by Lgr5; they drive the high turnover of the gut epithelium.","k":"Lgr5.","a":["lgr5","lgr-5","lgr 5"],"rn":[["","Which protein characterises intestinal stem cells?","Which protein characterises intestinal stem cells?","Lgr5"]]},{"id":14017,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Which epithelial cell shapes are most robust against damage?","o":["Squamous cells","Columnar cells","Cuboidal cells","Ciliated columnar cells","Goblet cells"],"e":"<b>Columnar and cuboidal</b> cells are not very robust; <b>squamous</b> cells are much <b>more robust</b> and withstand more damage.","w":["Less robust.","Less robust.","Columnar.","Columnar."],"k":"Squamous most robust.","rn":[["","Which epithelial cells are more robust?","Which epithelial cells are more robust?","Squamous; Withstand more damage"]]},{"id":14018,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Why does the ability of epithelium to renew carry a “risk”?","o":["Division can become uncontrolled (adenoma/carcinoma)","Division can stop altogether, giving atrophy of the epithelium","Division can make cells post-mitotic","Division can remove nuclei from cells","Division can cause inflammation to stop"],"e":"Renewal = <b>cell proliferation</b>; with lots of division comes a risk that division becomes <b>uncontrolled</b>: <b>benign adenomas</b>, and <b>malignant</b> transformation – <b>carcinomas</b> or <b>adenocarcinomas</b>. Inflammation and constant damage increase the risk.","w":["Not stated.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Renewal → risk of tumours.","rn":[["","Why does epithelial renewal carry a risk?","Why does epithelial renewal carry a risk?","Lots of cell division; Risk of uncontrolled division"]]},{"id":14019,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"How does coeliac disease fit the model of epithelial disease?","o":["Autoimmune damage outpaces renewal","Uncontrolled division produces a benign adenoma","Renewal outpaces damage, causing hyperplasia","Keratin is lost, making the gut permeable","Lysosomes lack hydrolases, causing inclusions"],"e":"<b>Damage</b> of the epithelium <b>faster than renewal capability</b> impairs organ function – e.g. <b>coeliac disease</b> due to an <b>autoimmune</b> response.","w":["Not.","Damage faster.","Not.","I-cell."],"k":"Coeliac: damage outpaces renewal.","rn":[["","What is the example of damage faster than renewal?","What is the example of damage faster than renewal?","Coeliac disease; Due to an autoimmune response"]]},{"id":14020,"t":"Muscle Tissue","f":"ma18","s":"A tissue shows parallel fibres with multiple nuclei and cross-striations. Another shows elongated “cigar-shaped” nuclei and no striations. Which are they?","o":["Skeletal muscle; smooth muscle","Smooth muscle; skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle; skeletal muscle","Nervous tissue; epithelium","Dense regular connective tissue; adipose tissue"],"e":"<b>Skeletal</b>: parallel fibres, <b>multiple nuclei</b>, <b>striated</b> (actin and myosin). <b>Smooth</b>: <b>no striation</b>, <b>elongated (cigar-shaped)</b> nuclei; outer layer of internal organs.","w":["Reversed.","Not cardiac first.","Not muscle.","Not muscle."],"k":"Skeletal striated multinucleate; smooth non-striated cigar nuclei.","rn":[["","Skeletal vs smooth: nuclei","Skeletal vs smooth: nuclei","Skeletal: multiple nuclei; Smooth: elongated (cigar-shaped)"],["","What are the features of skeletal muscle cells?","What are the features of skeletal muscle cells?","Parallel cells; Multiple nuclei; Striated"],["","What are the 3 types of muscle tissue?","What are the 3 types of muscle tissue?","Skeletal; Smooth; Cardiac"]]},{"id":14021,"t":"Muscle Tissue","s":"Where is smooth muscle positioned in an internal organ relative to the epithelium?","o":["Outside it, as the organ’s outer layer","Inside the epithelium, lining the lumen","Within the epithelium, between the cells","Only in skin, beneath the epidermis","Only in limbs, around the bones"],"e":"Looking from the inside of an organ outwards, smooth muscle comes <b>after the epithelium</b> – the outer layer of internal organs (GI tract, vessels, uterus).","w":["Outside.","Not.","Not.","Skeletal."],"k":"Smooth muscle outside epithelium.","rn":[["","What are the features of smooth muscle?","What are the features of smooth muscle?","Outer layer of internal organs; No striation; Elongated nuclei"]]},{"id":14022,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"What defines connective tissue?","o":["A defined extracellular matrix","Tightly packed cells with little matrix","Contractile proteins in the cytoplasm","Electrical excitability of the cells","Keratin layers on the surface"],"e":"Connective tissue is characterised by the presence of a <b>defined extracellular matrix</b>.","w":["Epithelium.","Muscle.","Nervous.","Skin."],"k":"Connective tissue = defined ECM.","rn":[["","Connective Tissue","Connective Tissue","Connects and protects; Defined extracellular matrix"]]},{"id":14023,"t":"Connective Tissue","f":"ma20","s":"Which classification of connective tissue is correct?","o":["Fibrous (loose, dense), specialised (bone, fat, cartilage), fluid","Fibrous (bone, cartilage), specialised (loose, dense), fluid (blood)","Fibrous (loose, dense), specialised (blood), fluid (adipose)","Epithelial, muscle and nervous","Squamous, cuboidal and columnar"],"e":"Connective tissue: <b>fibrous</b> (loose: areolar, reticular; dense: regular, irregular, elastic), <b>specialised</b> (bone: compact, spongy; adipose; cartilage: hyaline, fibrocartilage), <b>fluid</b> (blood).","w":["Swapped.","Blood is fluid.","Tissue types.","Epithelium."],"k":"Connective tissue classification.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of specialised connective tissue?","What are the 3 types of specialised connective tissue?","Bone; Adipose; Cartilage"],["","What are the 2 types of fibrous connective tissue?","What are the 2 types of fibrous connective tissue?","Loose; Dense"],["","What are the 3 main classes of connective tissue?","What are the 3 main classes of connective tissue?","Fibrous; Specialised; Fluid"]]},{"id":14024,"t":"Connective Tissue","f":"ma22","s":"Where are dense regular and dense irregular connective tissues found, and how do they differ?","o":["Regular: tendons, one orientation; irregular: dermis, many directions","Regular: dermis, one orientation; irregular: tendons, many directions","Regular: tendons, many directions; irregular: dermis, one orientation","Regular: spleen, reticular fibres; irregular: lymph nodes","Both: beneath the skin with sparse fibres"],"e":"<b>Dense regular</b>: <b>ligaments, tendons</b> – tightly packed type I collagen in the <b>same orientation</b>. <b>Dense irregular</b>: <b>glands, dermis</b> – type I collagen in <b>multiple directions</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Swapped.","Reticular.","Loose."],"k":"Dense regular vs irregular.","rn":[["","Regular vs irregular: fibre direction","Regular vs irregular: fibre direction","Regular: same orientation; Irregular: multiple directions"]]},{"id":14025,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which statement describes loose connective tissue?","o":["Under skin, around vessels; many cells, sparse type I collagen","Under skin, around vessels; few cells, dense type I collagen","In tendons; many cells, sparse type I collagen","Under skin, around vessels; many cells, type II collagen","A fluid tissue with cells in plasma"],"e":"<b>Loose</b> connective tissue: beneath the skin, around blood vessels, muscles and nerves; <b>high number of cells</b>; <b>sparse, randomly arranged collagen (type I)</b> and some elastic fibres; <b>binds tissues together</b>.","w":["Many cells, sparse.","Tendons are dense.","Type I.","Blood."],"k":"Loose CT features.","rn":[["","Loose connective tissue","Loose connective tissue","Many cells; Sparse, random type I collagen; Some elastic fibres"],["","Where is loose connective tissue found?","Where is loose connective tissue found?","Beneath the skin; Around blood vessels; Around muscles and nerves"]]},{"id":14026,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Reticular connective tissue supports soft organs. Which statement is correct?","o":["Type III fibres supporting spleen and liver; needs special stains","Many fibroblasts and type I fibres supporting spleen and liver; seen on H&E","Few fibroblasts, type III fibres, forms tendons; seen on H&E","Many fibroblasts, type IV sheets, supports spleen; needs special stains","A fluid tissue supporting lymph nodes"],"e":"<b>Reticular</b>: very many cells and <b>reticular fibres (type III collagen)</b>; supports soft organs – <b>spleen, lymph nodes, liver</b>; fibres <b>cannot be distinguished in H&E</b>.","w":["Type III; not seen on H&E.","Tendons.","Type III fibres.","Not fluid."],"k":"Reticular CT: type III, lymphoid organs.","rn":[["","Where is reticular connective tissue found?","Where is reticular connective tissue found?","Spleen; Lymph nodes; Liver"],["","Reticular connective tissue","Reticular connective tissue","Very many fibroblasts; Reticular fibres (type III); In ground substance"],["","Can you see reticular fibres on H&E?","Can you see reticular fibres on H&E?","No; Need different staining methods"]]},{"id":14027,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which pairing of collagen type and tissue is correct?","o":["Type II – cartilage","Type I – basement membranes","Type IV – tendon","Type III – cartilage","Type V – bone"],"e":"<b>Type I</b>: large fibres – skin, tendon, bone, ligaments. <b>Type II</b>: small fibres – <b>cartilage</b>. <b>Type III</b>: small fibres – vessels, parenchymal organs, marrow, lymphoid tissue. <b>Type IV</b>: sheet-like – basement membranes. <b>Type V</b>: thin fibrils – basement membranes (smooth, skeletal muscle).","w":["Type IV.","Type I.","Type II.","Type I."],"k":"Collagen types I–V.","rn":[["","Which tissues have collagen types I to IV?","Which tissues have collagen types I to IV?","I: skin/bone, II: cartilage; III: vessels; IV: basement membrane; MNEMONIC"],["","Type II collagen: tissue","Type II collagen: tissue","Cartilage; Also collagens IX, X, XI"],["","Type II collagen: morphology","Type II collagen: morphology","Small fibres"]]},{"id":14028,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which collagen type is the majority in the body and the primary collagen of dense connective tissue?","o":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type IV","Type V"],"e":"The <b>majority</b> of collagen in the body is <b>type I</b>, the primary collagen of dense regular and irregular connective tissue.","w":["Cartilage.","Reticular.","Basement membrane.","Basement membrane."],"k":"Type I = most abundant.","rn":[["","What is the majority type of collagen in the body?","What is the majority type of collagen in the body?","Type I"]]},{"id":14029,"t":"H&E Staining","s":"Can collagen types be distinguished on H&E?","o":["No – they look the same; location tells you the type","Yes – each type stains a different shade of pink","Yes – type I is purple and type II pink","No – collagen does not stain on H&E","Yes – type IV stains blue"],"e":"In H&E you <b>cannot tell collagen types apart</b>; from the <b>location</b> you can know which type is mostly there.","w":["Same.","Not.","It stains pink.","Not."],"k":"Collagen types: by location, not H&E.","rn":[["","Can H&E tell you the type of collagen?","Can H&E tell you the type of collagen?","No; It all looks the same"],["","How can you know the collagen type on H&E?","How can you know the collagen type on H&E?","By the location"]]},{"id":14030,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which statement about collagen structure is correct?","o":["α chains wound into triple helices, made by fibroblasts, cross-linked","A single α chain, made by fibroblasts, cross-linked into fibres","α chains wound into double helices, made by fibroblasts","α chains wound into triple helices, made by red blood cells","A carbohydrate polymer made by fibroblasts"],"e":"Collagens are a family of proteins; there are <b>at least 28 types of α chains</b> from different genes; precursor α chains form rigid <b>triple helices</b>, secreted mainly by <b>fibroblasts</b>, assembled into <b>cross-linked fibres and bundles</b>.","w":["Triple helix.","Triple.","Fibroblasts.","Protein."],"k":"Collagen: triple helix, fibroblasts.","rn":[["","What happens to collagen triple helices after the cell secretes them?","What happens to collagen triple helices after the cell secretes them?","Assembled into long filaments; Cross-linked into fibres and bundles"]]},{"id":14031,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which statement about the basement membrane is correct?","o":["It is a membrane and is acellular","It is a layer of flattened cells","It contains type II collagen mainly","It is visible as purple dots on H&E","It is part of the cytoplasm"],"e":"“Always remember that the <b>basement membrane</b> is a membrane. It's <b>acellular</b>.” It contains type IV and V collagen.","w":["Acellular.","IV and V.","Acellular.","Not."],"k":"Basement membrane is acellular.","rn":[["","Is the basement membrane cellular?","Is the basement membrane cellular?","No, acellular"]]},{"id":14032,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"What is the main cell of fibrous connective tissue, and what does it do?","o":["The fibroblast, producing collagen fibres","The fibroblast, producing mucus","The goblet cell, producing collagen fibres","The keratinocyte, producing keratin","The chondrocyte, producing collagen fibres"],"e":"“The one and only” cell of connective tissue (class question) is the <b>fibroblast</b>, which makes <b>collagen</b> fibres; fibroblasts self-renew, allowing healing.","w":["Collagen.","Goblet cells make mucus.","Skin epithelium.","Cartilage."],"k":"Fibroblast makes collagen.","rn":[["","Fibroblast","Fibroblast","Cell that makes the fibres; Makes collagen"],["","Which cells lie between collagen fibres in dense connective tissue?","Which cells lie between collagen fibres in dense connective tissue?","Fibroblasts; They make collagen"],["","What does fibrous connective tissue consist of?","What does fibrous connective tissue consist of?","Fibroblasts; Protein fibres (e.g. collagen)"]]},{"id":14033,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which functions of specialised connective tissues are correct?","o":["Bone/cartilage: strength, repair; adipose: insulation, energy","Bone/cartilage: insulation, energy store; adipose: strength, stability, repair","Bone/cartilage: hormone secretion; adipose: oxygen transport","Bone/cartilage: diffusion; adipose: absorption","Bone/cartilage: strength; adipose: diffusion and filtration"],"e":"<b>Bone, cartilage</b>: strength, stability, repair. <b>Adipose</b>: protection for internal organs, insulation, energy store. <b>Fibrous</b> (e.g. tendon): fibroblasts + collagen; repair.","w":["Swapped.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Specialised CT roles.","rn":[["","What do bone and cartilage provide?","What do bone and cartilage provide?","Strength; Stability; Repair"],["","What are the 3 functions of adipose tissue?","What are the 3 functions of adipose tissue?","Protection for internal organs; Insulation; Energy store"],["","What are the 3 types of specialised connective tissue?","What are the 3 types of specialised connective tissue?","Bone; Adipose; Cartilage"]]},{"id":14034,"t":"Connective Tissue","f":"ma24","s":"Why is blood classed as a connective tissue, and what are its components?","o":["Fluid connective tissue: white and red cells, proteins, fibrin, plasma","Fluid connective tissue: red cells and plasma only","Fluid epithelium: white and red cells, proteins, fibrin, plasma","A muscle tissue: red cells, proteins and plasma","A specialised connective tissue with no cells"],"e":"Blood is a <b>fluid connective tissue</b>: <b>white cells</b> (defence), <b>red cells</b> (oxygen), <b>proteins</b>, <b>fibrin</b> (haemostasis), and <b>plasma</b> (non-cellular, transports nutrients and molecules).","w":["More.","Connective.","Not.","Cells."],"k":"Blood = fluid CT.","rn":[["","What are the 2 cell components of blood?","What are the 2 cell components of blood?","White blood cells; Red blood cells"],["","What is the fluid connective tissue?","What is the fluid connective tissue?","Blood"]]},{"id":14035,"t":"Nervous Tissue","f":"ma25","s":"Why is nervous tissue not routinely studied histologically?","o":["Lack of samples (mostly post-mortem)","It does not stain with H&E at all","It contains no cells to examine","It is too large to section","It looks identical to muscle"],"e":"Nervous tissue is <b>not routinely studied histologically</b> because of a <b>lack of samples</b>: pathological nervous tissue is essentially all <b>post-mortem</b>, and quite damaged if there is pathology.","w":["Not stated.","Neurons and glia.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Nervous tissue: few samples.","rn":[["","Why is nervous tissue rarely studied histologically?","Why is nervous tissue rarely studied histologically?","Lack of samples; Mostly post-mortem and damaged"]]},{"id":14036,"t":"Glands","f":"ma27","s":"Which statement about glands is correct?","o":["Built from epithelium; exocrine via ducts, endocrine ductless into blood","Built from connective tissue; exocrine via ducts, endocrine into blood","Built from epithelium; exocrine into blood, endocrine via ducts","Built from muscle; exocrine via ducts, endocrine into blood","Built from epithelium; all secrete via ducts"],"e":"Glands are “constructed” from <b>epithelial cells</b> (secretory epithelial cells). <b>Exocrine</b>: secretions via <b>ducts</b> onto surfaces/cavities (sweat, saliva, milk). <b>Endocrine</b>: <b>ductless</b>, secrete <b>directly into blood</b>.","w":["Epithelium.","Swapped.","Epithelium.","Endocrine ductless."],"k":"Exocrine (ducts) vs endocrine (blood).","rn":[["","Endocrine gland","Endocrine gland","Ductless gland; Secretes directly into blood"],["","Exocrine Gland","Exocrine Gland","Secretions transported via ducts; Into cavities or surfaces"]]},{"id":14037,"t":"Glands","s":"Name the unicellular gland that secretes mucus in the gut and airways.","e":"The goblet cell is a unicellular mucus-secreting gland; mucus appears clear on H&E.","k":"Goblet cell.","a":["goblet cell","goblet cells","goblet"],"rn":[["","Unicellular Gland","Unicellular Gland","One cell; e.g. goblet cells"],["","What do goblet cells produce?","What do goblet cells produce?","Mucus"]]},{"id":14038,"t":"H&E Staining","s":"On H&E, columnar cells secrete a pink substance while other cells contain clear material. What do these suggest?","o":["Pink = protein; clear = mucus","Pink = mucus; clear = protein","Pink = DNA; clear = mucus","Pink = protein; clear = fat","Pink = keratin; clear = mucus"],"e":"“<b>Pink signifies proteins</b>”, whereas <b>mucus</b> looks <b>clear</b> on H&E.","w":["Swapped.","Purple.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Pink = protein; clear = mucus.","rn":[["","How does mucus appear on H&E?","How does mucus appear on H&E?","Clear (not pink)"]]},{"id":14039,"t":"Glands","f":"ma29","s":"Which mode of secretion disrupts the whole cell, requiring high cell turnover?","o":["Holocrine","Apocrine","Merocrine","Endocrine","Serous"],"e":"<b>Merocrine</b>: secretes without membrane disruption. <b>Apocrine</b>: vesicles take part of the membrane. <b>Holocrine</b>: the <b>whole cell is disrupted</b>, so high turnover/renewal is needed. Memory aid: mero = merely, apo = a part, holo = whole.","w":["Part of membrane.","No disruption.","Ductless.","Protein."],"k":"Holocrine = whole cell.","rn":[["","Why does holocrine secretion need high turnover?","Why does holocrine secretion need high turnover?","Whole cell is disrupted; Needs renewal"]]},{"id":14040,"t":"Glands","s":"Which mode of secretion releases products in vesicles that take part of the cell membrane with them?","o":["Apocrine","Merocrine","Holocrine","Endocrine","Mucous"],"e":"<b>Apocrine</b>: secretes in vesicles that <b>take part of the membrane</b>.","w":["No disruption.","Whole cell.","Not a mode.","Gland type."],"k":"Apocrine = part of membrane.","rn":[["","Apocrine","Apocrine","Secretion takes part of membrane; Released as vesicles"]]},{"id":14041,"t":"Glands","s":"Which pairing of gland type and secretion is correct?","o":["Serous – protein; mucous – mucus; sebaceous – sebum","Serous – mucus; mucous – protein; sebaceous – sebum","Serous – protein; mucous – mucus; sebaceous – hormones","Serous – sebum; mucous – mucus; sebaceous – protein","Serous – protein; mucous – sebum; sebaceous – mucus"],"e":"<b>Serous</b> glands: <b>protein</b>. <b>Mucous</b>: <b>mucus</b>. <b>Sebaceous</b>: skin specific (<b>sebum</b>).","w":["Swapped.","Sebum.","Swapped.","Swapped."],"k":"Serous, mucous, sebaceous.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of gland by secretion?","What are the 3 types of gland by secretion?","Serous; Mucous; Sebaceous"]]},{"id":14042,"t":"Glands","s":"Name the contractile cells that squeeze secretions out of a branched gland.","e":"Myoepithelial cells expel secretions from branched glands.","k":"Myoepithelial cells.","a":["myoepithelial cells","myoepithelial cell","myoepithelial","myoepithelium"],"rn":[["","What do myoepithelial cells do?","What do myoepithelial cells do?","Expel secretions"]]},{"id":14043,"t":"Glands","f":"mb29","s":"What are acini?","o":["Rounded clusters of secretory cells","Rounded clusters of myoepithelial cells","Branched ducts draining a gland","Hair-like projections of the epithelium","Collagen bundles around a gland"],"e":"<b>Acini</b>: rounded clusters of secretory cells (e.g. protein-secreting cells, labelled A and P on the figure).","w":["Secretory.","Ducts.","Cilia.","Not."],"k":"Acini = secretory clusters.","rn":[["","Where are the secretory cells in the \"single secretory cells\" gland type?","Where are the secretory cells in the \"single secretory cells\" gland type?","Scattered in the surface epithelium; Between the other epithelial cells"],["","What are glands constructed from?","What are glands constructed from?","Epithelial cells; Including secretory epithelial cells"],["","Which 2 gland structures are simple?","Which 2 gland structures are simple?","Single secretory cells; Straight tubular gland"]]},{"id":14044,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"What is autophagy?","o":["Self-eating: digesting cytosol and worn-out organelles via lysosomes","Self-eating: digesting cytosol and organelles without lysosomes","Programmed cell death with enzymes acting in the cytoplasm","Uptake of external molecules by endocytosis","Self-renewal of tissue by stem cells"],"e":"<b>Autophagy</b>: digestion of <b>cytosol, molecules (proteins) and worn-out organelles</b>, completed by <b>lysosomes</b>. All cells can do it; it maintains cell health.","w":["Lysosomes.","Apoptosis.","Endosomes.","Renewal."],"k":"Autophagy = self-eating via lysosomes.","rn":[["","Autophagy","Autophagy","\"Self-eating\"; Digests cytosol, molecules; Worn-out organelles"]]},{"id":14045,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"When is autophagy non-selective, and when selective?","o":["Non-selective in starvation; selective in growth or for worn-out organelles","Selective in starvation; non-selective in growth or for worn-out organelles","Always non-selective, whatever the trigger","Always selective, whatever the trigger","Non-selective in growth; never selective"],"e":"Autophagy is <b>non-selective</b> in <b>starvation</b>, and <b>selective</b> to remove components during <b>cell growth or differentiation</b> or worn-out/surplus organelles (mitochondria, peroxisomes, ribosomes). It also matters in <b>innate immunity</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Can be selective.","Non-selective in starvation.","Can be selective."],"k":"Non-selective (starvation) vs selective.","rn":[["","When is selective autophagy used?","When is selective autophagy used?","Cell growth or differentiation; Remove worn-out/surplus organelles"]]},{"id":14046,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"Which statement contrasts autophagy and apoptosis correctly?","o":["Autophagy: cell usually survives; apoptosis: irreversible death","Autophagy: irreversible death; apoptosis: uses autophagosomes, cell survives","Both use autophagosomes and lysosomes","Apoptosis is reversible; autophagy is not","Autophagy always kills; apoptosis needs lysosomes"],"e":"<b>Autophagy</b>: maintenance; membrane encircles target → autophagosome → fuses with lysosome; cell usually survives (excessive damage can trigger death). <b>Apoptosis</b>: <b>programmed cell death</b>; enzymes act in the <b>cytoplasm</b>, no lysosomes or autophagosomes; <b>no going back</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Apoptosis uses neither.","Irreversible.","Not."],"k":"Autophagy vs apoptosis.","rn":[["","Apoptosis","Apoptosis","Programmed cell death; No going back once started"],["","Is autophagy always linked to cell death?","Is autophagy always linked to cell death?","No; Death if excessive damage"],["","Autophagy vs apoptosis: machinery","Autophagy vs apoptosis: machinery","Autophagy: lysosomes and autophagosomes; Apoptosis: enzymes in cytoplasm"]]},{"id":14047,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"Which statement about lysosomes is correct?","o":["Acid hydrolases breaking down proteins, nucleic acids, lipids, sugars at low pH","Alkaline hydrolases breaking down proteins, nucleic acids, lipids at high pH","Acid hydrolases breaking down collagen only at low pH","DNA polymerases replicating DNA at low pH","Identical organelles with no enzymes"],"e":"Lysosomes contain <b>acid hydrolases</b> that break down <b>proteins, nucleic acids, lipids, sugars</b>; <b>low pH</b>; <b>heterogeneous</b> organelles.","w":["Low pH.","Not only.","Not.","Heterogeneous."],"k":"Lysosomes: acid hydrolases, low pH.","rn":[["","What 4 biomolecules can acid hydrolases break down?","What 4 biomolecules can acid hydrolases break down?","Proteins; Nucleic acids; Lipids; Sugars"]]},{"id":14048,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"What are melanosomes?","o":["Lysosome-type organelles holding melanin for secretion","Lysosome-type organelles digesting melanin by autophagy","Mitochondria in melanocytes making melanin","Autophagosomes in neurons","Vesicles in goblet cells"],"e":"<b>Melanosomes</b> are a special category of lysosomes in <b>melanocytes</b>, holding <b>melanin</b>, secreted by <b>exocytosis</b> for <b>skin pigmentation</b>.","w":["Secreted.","Lysosome-type.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Melanosomes = melanin lysosomes.","rn":[["","Melanosome","Melanosome","Special lysosome; In melanocytes; Holds melanin pigments"]]},{"id":14049,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","f":"ma33","s":"Put the steps of autophagic degradation in order.","o":["Double membrane → autophagosome → lysosome → degradation","Lysosome → double membrane → autophagosome → degradation of contents","Double membrane → lysosome → autophagosome → degradation","Endosome → autophagosome → lysosome → degradation","Degradation → double membrane → autophagosome"],"e":"A portion of cytoplasm is surrounded by a <b>double membrane</b> → <b>autophagosome</b> → <b>fuses with a lysosome</b> → <b>degradation</b>. Formation is tightly controlled and involves metabolic signalling. The autophagosome is <b>not</b> itself a lysosome.","w":["Membrane first.","Autophagosome before fusion.","Inside rubbish.","Order."],"k":"Autophagy pathway steps.","rn":[["","Autophagosome","Autophagosome","Cytoplasm portion; In a double membrane"],["","What happens when the autophagosome fuses with a lysosome?","What happens when the autophagosome fuses with a lysosome?","Biomolecule or organelle degradation"]]},{"id":14050,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"How do external molecules taken in by endocytosis reach lysosomes?","o":["Endosomes fuse with lysosomes to form endolysosomes","Endosomes form autophagosomes with double membranes","Endosomes enter the nucleus for digestion","Endosomes are secreted straight back out","Endosomes bypass lysosomes entirely"],"e":"For material entering by <b>endocytosis</b>, <b>endosomes</b> fuse with lysosomes forming <b>endolysosomes</b>. Inside rubbish → autophagosome; outside rubbish → endosome.","w":["Inside rubbish.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Endosome + lysosome = endolysosome.","rn":[["","How do lysosomes deal with things entering by endocytosis?","How do lysosomes deal with things entering by endocytosis?","Endosomes fuse with lysosomes; Creating endolysosomes"]]},{"id":14051,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"Impaired autophagy of mitochondria is linked to early-onset Parkinson’s. Name this selective process.","e":"Mitophagy is selective autophagy of mitochondria.","k":"Mitophagy.","a":["mitophagy"],"rn":[["","Impaired mitophagy is linked to what?","Impaired mitophagy is linked to what?","Early onset Parkinson's"],["","Mitophagy","Mitophagy","Removing damaged mitochondria; By autophagy"]]},{"id":14052,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"Which statement about lysosomal disorders is correct?","o":["Rare, genetic, mainly recessive, with early severe symptoms","Common, acquired, mainly dominant, with late mild symptoms","Rare, genetic, mainly dominant, with late mild symptoms","Common, genetic, mainly recessive, affecting skin only","Rare, acquired, caused by excess autophagy"],"e":"Lysosomal disorders are <b>rare</b>, <b>genetic</b>, mainly <b>inherited and recessive</b>; cells cannot remove toxic by-products or exhausted organelles, so symptoms appear <b>early</b> and can be <b>severe</b>, with variability depending on cells affected and degree of impairment.","w":["Rare.","Recessive, early.","Not.","Genetic."],"k":"Lysosomal disorders: rare, genetic, recessive.","rn":[["","I-cell disease","I-cell disease","Very severe, rare; Lysosomal metabolic disease; Genetic cause"],["","When do symptoms of lysosomal disorders appear?","When do symptoms of lysosomal disorders appear?","Early"]]},{"id":14053,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"A child has a disorder in which acid hydrolases are made but secreted from the cell instead of entering lysosomes. Name the disease.","e":"I-cell (inclusion-cell) disease: hydrolases are secreted rather than delivered to lysosomes, so undigested molecules accumulate.","k":"I-cell disease.","a":["i-cell disease","i cell disease","inclusion cell disease","inclusion-cell disease","icell disease","mucolipidosis ii"],"rn":[["","Why can hydrolases not work in I-cell disease?","Why can hydrolases not work in I-cell disease?","Made but cannot enter lysosomes; Secreted instead"],["","What is missing from lysosomes in I-cell disease?","What is missing from lysosomes in I-cell disease?","Acid hydrolases"]]},{"id":14054,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"Name the protein whose accumulation in neurodegenerative disorders has been attributed to declining autophagy.","e":"Declining autophagic removal has been linked to tau accumulation in neurodegeneration; autophagy declines with age.","k":"Tau.","a":["tau","tau protein","tau proteins"],"rn":[["","Impaired autophagy causes what?","Impaired autophagy causes what?","Accumulation (e.g. tau)"],["","Which protein accumulates in neurodegenerative disorders?","Which protein accumulates in neurodegenerative disorders?","Tau proteins"],["","Why do tau proteins accumulate?","Why do tau proteins accumulate?","Decline in autophagic removal"]]},{"id":14055,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"How does autophagy change with ageing?","o":["It gradually declines","It increases sharply","It stays constant","It stops at birth","It only begins in old age"],"e":"During <b>ageing</b>, autophagy <b>gradually declines</b>, like many physiological functions.","w":["Declines.","Declines.","Present.","Present."],"k":"Autophagy declines with age.","rn":[["","What happens to autophagy with ageing?","What happens to autophagy with ageing?","Gradually declines"]]},{"id":14056,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"This epithelium lines an organ that must repeatedly stretch and empty, and has a variable number of layers. Name it.","e":"Transitional epithelium (bladder, urethra): a variable number of layers that allows stretching and is elastic, with surface, middle and basal layers.","k":"Transitional.","img":{"src":"14/transitional","cap":"Lining of a hollow organ, H&amp;E. S, M and B = surface, middle and basal layers."},"a":["transitional","transitional epithelium","urothelium"],"rn":[["","Transitional Epithelium","Transitional Epithelium","Variable number of cell layers; Elastic epithelium"]]},{"id":14057,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","f":"ma04","s":"Why is microscopic anatomy described as a “bridge”?","o":["It links physiology with gross anatomy and disease","It replaces gross anatomy in modern medical teaching","It studies whole organs without microscopes","It is used only in research laboratories","It is unrelated to disease"],"e":"Microscopic anatomy is a <b>bridge</b> between cartoon physiology and gross anatomy, and helps understand <b>how disease pathology emerges</b> – linking cells, tissue structure, function and pathology.","w":["Bridge.","Microscopes.","Clinical.","Disease."],"k":"Histology bridges physiology and anatomy."},{"id":14058,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"What is mesothelium?","o":["Squamous epithelium lining membranes","Squamous epithelium lining vessels","Columnar epithelium lining the gut","Transitional epithelium lining the bladder","Keratinised epithelium of skin"],"e":"<b>Mesothelium</b>: squamous epithelium of <b>membranes</b>. Endothelium lines vessels.","w":["Endothelium.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Mesothelium lines membranes.","rn":[["","What is squamous epithelium called in vessels and membranes?","What is squamous epithelium called in vessels and membranes?","Vessels: endothelium; Membranes: mesothelium"],["","Endothelium","Endothelium","Squamous epithelium lining blood vessels"]]},{"id":14059,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"A biopsy shows a single layer of flat cells lining a capillary. Which description is most accurate?","o":["Endothelium","Mesothelium","Simple cuboidal epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Pseudostratified columnar"],"e":"Flat cells lining a vessel = <b>endothelium</b>, the squamous-type lining of blood vessels.","w":["Membranes.","Not flat.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Vessel lining = endothelium.","rn":[["","Endothelium","Endothelium","Squamous epithelium lining blood vessels"]]},{"id":14060,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Which epithelium would you expect lining the small intestine, with microvilli for absorption?","o":["Simple columnar epithelium","Stratified squamous keratinised","Transitional","Simple squamous","Pseudostratified ciliated"],"e":"<b>Columnar</b> epithelium lines the <b>GI tract</b> (absorption, secretion), with <b>microvilli</b> increasing surface area in the gut.","w":["Skin.","Bladder.","Vessels/lungs.","Trachea."],"k":"Gut = simple columnar with microvilli.","rn":[["","Which epithelium: GI tract?","Which epithelium: GI tract?","Simple columnar"]]},{"id":14061,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"A patient has a mutation preventing hydrolases from entering lysosomes. Which organelle process fails, and what accumulates?","o":["Lysosomal degradation fails; undigested biomolecules accumulate","Autophagosome formation fails; organelles accumulate in the nucleus","Lysosomal degradation fails; hydrolases accumulate in lysosomes","Collagen synthesis fails; fibres accumulate","Mitophagy fails; mitochondria divide excessively"],"e":"This is <b>I-cell disease</b>: hydrolases are secreted instead of entering lysosomes, so lysosomal digestion fails and <b>undigested biomolecules</b> form large <b>inclusions</b>.","w":["Lysosomes.","Hydrolases are secreted.","Not.","Not."],"k":"I-cell mechanism.","rn":[["","What forms in cells in I-cell disease?","What forms in cells in I-cell disease?","Large inclusions; Of undigested biomolecules"],["","What do lysosomal storage diseases cause?","What do lysosomal storage diseases cause?","Accumulation of undigested biomolecules"]]},{"id":14062,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Which tissue type is described as the main functional tissue of internal organs?","o":["Epithelial tissue","Connective tissue","Muscle tissue","Nervous tissue","Adipose tissue"],"e":"Summary: epithelial tissue consists of three cell types in various arrangements; it is the <b>main functional tissue type of internal organs</b>.","w":["Supports.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Connective."],"k":"Epithelium = main functional tissue.","rn":[["","Epithelium is the main functional tissue of what?","Epithelium is the main functional tissue of what?","Internal organs"]]},{"id":14063,"t":"Summary","s":"Which two tissue types can self-renew and achieve tissue repair?","o":["Epithelial and connective tissue","Epithelial and nervous tissue","Connective and muscle tissue","Muscle and nervous tissue","Epithelial tissue only"],"e":"Summary: both <b>epithelial and connective</b> tissue can <b>self-renew</b> and achieve <b>tissue repair</b> (fibroblasts self-renew – otherwise we couldn't heal a cut).","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Connective too."],"k":"Epithelium and CT self-renew.","rn":[["","Which 2 tissue types can self-renew and repair?","Which 2 tissue types can self-renew and repair?","Epithelial; Connective"]]},{"id":14064,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Which statement about tissue renewal and cancer risk applies to both epithelium and connective tissue?","o":["Self-renewing tissue always risks uncontrolled renewal","Self-renewing tissue is protected from cancer by its turnover","Self-renewing tissue cannot become malignant","Only nervous tissue risks uncontrolled growth","Only blood carries any renewal risk"],"e":"“Self-renewing tissue always carries the <b>risk that self-renewal becomes uncontrolled</b>.”","w":["Risk.","It can.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Renewal carries risk.","rn":[["","Why does epithelial renewal carry a risk?","Why does epithelial renewal carry a risk?","Lots of cell division; Risk of uncontrolled division"],["","Why can connective tissue self-renew?","Why can connective tissue self-renew?","Fibroblasts self-renew; Make more collagen"],["","Which 2 tissue types can self-renew and repair?","Which 2 tissue types can self-renew and repair?","Epithelial; Connective"]]},{"id":14065,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"A tissue from the respiratory tract has tall cells with cilia and nuclei at different heights, plus goblet cells. Which combination is present?","o":["Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells","Stratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells","Pseudostratified ciliated cuboidal with sebaceous glands","Transitional epithelium with serous glands","Simple squamous with apocrine glands"],"e":"<b>Pseudostratified ciliated columnar</b> epithelium lines the trachea; <b>goblet cells</b> are <b>unicellular</b> mucus-secreting glands; cilia propel mucus.","w":["Pseudostratified.","Columnar.","Bladder.","Not."],"k":"Airway epithelium + goblet cells.","rn":[["","Why is pseudostratified only columnar?","Why is pseudostratified only columnar?","Columnar cells are tall; And rectangular"],["","Which cells form pseudostratified epithelium?","Which cells form pseudostratified epithelium?","Columnar cells"],["","What is the subtype of pseudostratified epithelium?","What is the subtype of pseudostratified epithelium?","Ciliated"]]},{"id":14066,"t":"Muscle Tissue","s":"Which statement about where skeletal and smooth muscle are found is correct?","o":["Skeletal: limbs and body walls; smooth: GI tract, vessels, uterus","Skeletal: GI tract and vessels; smooth: limbs and body walls","Skeletal: limbs and uterus; smooth: GI tract and vessels","Skeletal and smooth: both only in the heart","Smooth: limbs only"],"e":"<b>Skeletal</b>: limbs, abdominal wall, thoracic wall. <b>Smooth</b>: internal organs – GI tract, blood vessels, uterus. Cardiac: heart.","w":["Swapped.","Uterus is smooth.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Muscle locations.","rn":[["","Where is smooth muscle found?","Where is smooth muscle found?","Internal organs; GI tract; Blood vessels; Uterus"],["","Where is skeletal muscle found?","Where is skeletal muscle found?","Limbs; Abdominal wall; Thoracic wall"]]},{"id":14067,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which collagen type would predominate in the basement membrane under an epithelium?","o":["Type IV (sheet-like)","Type I (large fibres)","Type II (small fibres)","Type III (reticular)","Type V (tendon)"],"e":"<b>Type IV</b>: sheet-like layer – <b>basement membranes</b>. Type V also appears in basement membranes (smooth, skeletal muscle).","w":["Tendons, skin.","Cartilage.","Lymphoid.","Basement membranes of muscle."],"k":"Basement membrane = type IV.","rn":[["","Type IV collagen: morphology","Type IV collagen: morphology","Sheet like layer"]]},{"id":14068,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"A lymph node is stained to show its supporting framework. Which fibres and collagen type, and why special stains?","o":["Reticular fibres (type III) – not seen in H&E","Reticular fibres (type III) – clearly seen in H&E","Large fibres (type I) – clearly seen in H&E","Sheet-like (type IV) – not seen in H&E","Small fibres (type II) – from cartilage"],"e":"Lymphoid tissue is supported by <b>reticular</b> connective tissue with <b>type III</b> collagen; reticular fibres <b>cannot be distinguished in H&E</b>, so other stains are needed.","w":["Not seen.","Reticular.","Basement membrane.","Cartilage."],"k":"Reticular fibres need special stains.","rn":[["","Reticular connective tissue","Reticular connective tissue","Very many fibroblasts; Reticular fibres (type III); In ground substance"]]},{"id":14069,"t":"Glands","s":"Which statement about endocrine glands is correct?","o":["Ductless; secrete directly into the blood","Ductless; secrete onto body surfaces","With ducts; secrete directly into the blood","With ducts; secrete onto body surfaces","Unicellular; secrete mucus"],"e":"<b>Endocrine</b> glands are <b>ductless</b>, releasing secretions <b>directly into the blood</b> (covered in a later module).","w":["Exocrine-like.","Ductless.","Exocrine.","Goblet."],"k":"Endocrine = ductless.","rn":[["","Endocrine gland","Endocrine gland","Ductless gland; Secretes directly into blood"]]},{"id":14070,"t":"Glands","s":"Which secretion mode would require the highest rate of gland cell renewal?","o":["Holocrine","Apocrine","Merocrine","Serous","Mucous"],"e":"<b>Holocrine</b> secretion disrupts the <b>whole cell</b>, so it needs <b>high turnover/renewal</b>.","w":["Partial.","None lost.","Product.","Product."],"k":"Holocrine → high turnover.","rn":[["","Why does holocrine secretion need high turnover?","Why does holocrine secretion need high turnover?","Whole cell is disrupted; Needs renewal"]]},{"id":14071,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"Which organelle would a cell use to remove its own exhausted mitochondria?","o":["An autophagosome fusing with a lysosome (mitophagy)","An endosome fusing with a lysosome (endocytosis)","A melanosome releasing contents by exocytosis","The nucleus digesting the mitochondria","A ribosome breaking down mitochondrial protein"],"e":"Exhausted mitochondria are removed by <b>mitophagy</b>: enclosed in an <b>autophagosome</b> that fuses with a <b>lysosome</b>.","w":["Outside rubbish.","Melanin.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Mitophagy via autophagosome.","rn":[["","Is an autophagosome a lysosome?","Is an autophagosome a lysosome?","No; It fuses with a lysosome"],["","What happens when the autophagosome fuses with a lysosome?","What happens when the autophagosome fuses with a lysosome?","Biomolecule or organelle degradation"]]},{"id":14072,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"A cell under prolonged nutrient deprivation begins to die after digesting its own contents. What process is described?","o":["Excessive autophagy leading to cell death","Selective autophagy preserving the cell","Apoptosis triggered by lysosomes","Holocrine secretion of the whole cell","Endocytosis of external nutrients"],"e":"In starvation cells digest their own contents for energy (non-selective autophagy); if the stress persists, cells start dying, “eating themselves”.","w":["Starvation, persistent.","Apoptosis lacks lysosomes.","Glands.","Not."],"k":"Excessive autophagy → death.","rn":[["","Is autophagy always linked to cell death?","Is autophagy always linked to cell death?","No; Death if excessive damage"],["","What happens if autophagy is excessive?","What happens if autophagy is excessive?","Programmed cell death is initiated"]]},{"id":14073,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"Which explanation of why autophagy capacity differs between cells is given?","o":["It depends on the cell’s lysosomal content","It depends on the cell’s number of nuclei","It depends on the type of collagen nearby","It depends on the number of cilia","Only neurons can do it"],"e":"Autophagy is a function of <b>all cells</b>; how much a cell can do depends on its <b>lysosomal content</b>.","w":["Not.","Not.","Not.","All cells."],"k":"Lysosome number limits autophagy.","rn":[["","Do all cells have lysosomes?","Do all cells have lysosomes?","Yes; Autophagy depends on lysosomal content"]]},{"id":14074,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which tissue would you expect to stain mostly purple on H&E?","o":["A densely cellular lymph node","A tendon with sparse fibroblasts","The dermis with dense collagen","A ligament with parallel collagen","A mucus-filled gland"],"e":"<b>Haematoxylin</b> stains nuclei purple, so densely cellular tissue (many nuclei) appears more purple. Tendons, ligaments and dermis are mostly pink collagen; mucus is clear.","w":["Mostly pink.","Pink.","Pink.","Clear."],"k":"More nuclei → more purple.","rn":[["","Where is reticular connective tissue found?","Where is reticular connective tissue found?","Spleen; Lymph nodes; Liver"]]},{"id":14075,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"A patient's skin injury heals with new collagen. Which cell and collagen type are mainly responsible?","o":["Fibroblasts making type I collagen","Fibroblasts making type II collagen","Keratinocytes making type I collagen","Goblet cells making type IV collagen","Melanocytes making type III collagen"],"e":"Connective tissue self-renews via <b>fibroblasts</b>, which make collagen; skin collagen is mainly <b>type I</b>.","w":["Cartilage.","Keratin.","Mucus.","Melanin."],"k":"Fibroblasts + type I collagen in repair.","rn":[["","Why can connective tissue self-renew?","Why can connective tissue self-renew?","Fibroblasts self-renew; Make more collagen"],["","Which cells lie between collagen fibres in dense connective tissue?","Which cells lie between collagen fibres in dense connective tissue?","Fibroblasts; They make collagen"],["","Fibroblast","Fibroblast","Cell that makes the fibres; Makes collagen"]]},{"id":14076,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Which statement best explains why epithelial damage in the GI tract and airways is common but recoverable?","o":["Less robust, but stem cells allow renewal","They are more robust cells, so damage is rarely lasting","They are keratinised, so damage is limited","They are post-mitotic, so damage is permanent","They are lined by mesothelium that renews"],"e":"GI and respiratory epithelia (columnar/cuboidal) are prone to damage, but <b>high turnover</b> via <b>stem/progenitor cells</b> enables renewal.","w":["Less robust.","Not.","They renew.","Not."],"k":"Prone to damage but renew.","rn":[["","What does epithelial self-renewal depend on?","What does epithelial self-renewal depend on?","Tissue-specific stem/progenitor cells"],["","Which epithelial cells are more robust?","Which epithelial cells are more robust?","Squamous; Withstand more damage"]]},{"id":14077,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Which pairing of organ and epithelium is <b>incorrect</b>?","o":["Bladder – pseudostratified ciliated","Skin – stratified keratinised squamous","Mouth – stratified non-keratinised squamous","Kidney tubules – simple cuboidal","Trachea – pseudostratified ciliated"],"e":"The <b>bladder</b> is lined by <b>transitional</b> epithelium.","w":["Correct.","Correct.","Correct.","Correct."],"k":"Bladder = transitional.","rn":[["","What is the subtype of pseudostratified epithelium?","What is the subtype of pseudostratified epithelium?","Ciliated"]]},{"id":14078,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which statement about adipose tissue is correct?","o":["Specialised CT: protection, insulation, energy","Specialised connective tissue: strength, stability and repair","Fluid connective tissue: transport of nutrients","Epithelial tissue: protection and insulation","Muscle tissue: heat production"],"e":"<b>Adipose tissue</b> (adipocytes) is <b>specialised connective tissue</b>: protection, insulation, energy store.","w":["Bone/cartilage.","Blood.","Connective.","Not."],"k":"Adipose = specialised CT.","rn":[["","What are the 3 functions of adipose tissue?","What are the 3 functions of adipose tissue?","Protection for internal organs; Insulation; Energy store"]]},{"id":14079,"t":"H&E Staining","s":"Which explanation of the term “basophilic” is correct?","o":["Acidic structures that attract basic dyes like haematoxylin","Basic structures that attract acidic dyes like eosin","Acidic structures that attract acidic dyes like eosin","Basic structures that attract basic dyes like haematoxylin","Structures that repel all dyes"],"e":"<b>Basophilic</b> = “base-loving”: acidic structures (e.g. DNA) that bind <b>basic</b> dyes such as haematoxylin (purple). Acidophilic structures bind eosin (pink).","w":["Acidophilic.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Basophilic = binds haematoxylin.","rn":[["","Haematoxylin","Haematoxylin","Basic dye; Stains acidic (basophilic) structures; Purple"]]},{"id":14080,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Name the motile surface projections labelled <b>A</b>, which have a core of microtubules.","e":"A = cilia: motile, microtubule core, move mucus. Microvilli have a microfilament core and increase surface area for absorption.","k":"A = cilia.","img":{"src":"14/pseudo","cap":"Airway lining, H&amp;E."},"a":["cilia","cilium","motile cilia"],"rn":[["","What is the core of cilia made of?","What is the core of cilia made of?","Microtubules / contractile elements"],["","What are the 3 specialisations of epithelial cells?","What are the 3 specialisations of epithelial cells?","Cilia; Microvilli; Keratin"],["","Cilia","Cilia","Apical specialisation of epithelial cells; Long, fine, hair-like, motile projections; Beat synchronously"]]},{"id":14081,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Which statement about keratin is correct?","o":["An intermediate filament on skin epithelium","A microtubule protein forming cilia","A collagen type in the dermis","A microfilament protein forming the core of microvilli","A lysosomal enzyme"],"e":"<b>Keratin</b>: layers of the intermediate filament protein on the apical surface of <b>skin</b> epithelial cells.","w":["Cilia.","Not collagen.","Microvilli.","Not."],"k":"Keratin = intermediate filament.","rn":[["","Keratin","Keratin","Intermediate filament protein layers; On apical skin epithelium"]]},{"id":14082,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which of the following would most likely contain type II collagen?","o":["Hyaline cartilage","Tendon","Skin","Basement membrane","Lymph node"],"e":"<b>Type II</b> (small fibres): <b>cartilage</b> (also collagens IX, X, XI).","w":["Type I.","Type I.","Type IV.","Type III."],"k":"Cartilage = type II.","rn":[["","Type II collagen: tissue","Type II collagen: tissue","Cartilage; Also collagens IX, X, XI"],["","What are the 2 types of cartilage on the classification?","What are the 2 types of cartilage on the classification?","Hyaline; Fibrocartilage"]]},{"id":14083,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Which statement about the elasticity of transitional epithelium is correct?","o":["It is elastic, where tissue must expand and contract","It is rigid, where tissue must resist stretch","It is keratinised, where tissue must be waterproof","It is ciliated, where mucus must be moved","It is always a single layer"],"e":"Transitional epithelium is quite <b>elastic</b>, found where tissues need to <b>expand and contract</b> (bladder, urethra).","w":["Elastic.","Skin.","Trachea.","Variable layers."],"k":"Transitional = elastic.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of dense connective tissue?","What are the 3 types of dense connective tissue?","Regular; Irregular; Elastic"],["","What are the 2 types of fibre in loose connective tissue?","What are the 2 types of fibre in loose connective tissue?","Collagen fibres; Elastic fibres"],["","Loose connective tissue","Loose connective tissue","Many cells; Sparse, random type I collagen; Some elastic fibres"]]},{"id":14084,"t":"Glands","s":"Which example best represents a whole-organ gland?","o":["The pancreas","A goblet cell","A single sweat duct cell","A fibroblast","A melanocyte"],"e":"A <b>whole organ</b> can be a gland, e.g. the <b>pancreas</b>.","w":["Unicellular.","Part of a gland.","CT.","Skin cell."],"k":"Pancreas = whole-organ gland.","rn":[["","Can a whole organ be a gland?","Can a whole organ be a gland?","Yes; e.g. the pancreas"]]},{"id":14085,"t":"Glands","s":"Which exocrine secretions are given as examples?","o":["Sweat, saliva and breast milk","Sweat, saliva and insulin","Insulin and thyroxine","Saliva, mucus and blood plasma","Collagen and keratin"],"e":"Exocrine secretions via ducts: <b>sweat, saliva, breast milk</b>.","w":["Insulin is endocrine.","Endocrine.","Plasma is not.","Not."],"k":"Exocrine examples.","rn":[["","What are 3 examples of exocrine secretions?","What are 3 examples of exocrine secretions?","Sweat; Saliva; Breast milk"]]},{"id":14086,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Name the structure labelled <b>A</b>, the basic unit of compact bone.","e":"A = an osteon (Haversian system), the basic unit of compact bone. Bone and cartilage give strength, stability and repair.","k":"A = osteon.","img":{"src":"14/bone","cap":"Ground section of compact bone."},"a":["osteon","osteons","haversian system","haversian"],"rn":[["","Osteon","Osteon","Basic unit of bone"]]},{"id":14087,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Name the cell labelled <b>A</b>, sitting in a space within a smooth, glassy matrix rich in type II collagen.","e":"A = chondrocyte, the cell of cartilage (type II collagen).","k":"A = chondrocyte.","img":{"src":"14/cartilage","cap":"Specialised connective tissue, H&amp;E."},"a":["chondrocyte","chondrocytes"],"rn":[["","Chondrocyte","Chondrocyte","Cartilage cell"]]},{"id":14088,"t":"Nervous Tissue","s":"Which statement about nervous tissue cells is correct?","o":["Neurons and glia; highly specialised","Neurons only, with no glial cells","Neurons and fibroblasts; it is a form of connective tissue","Glia only; routinely biopsied","Neurons and glia; routinely biopsied"],"e":"Nervous tissue: <b>neurons</b> and <b>glial cells</b> (astrocytes, oligodendrocytes); <b>highly specialised</b>. Cortex figure labels: C blood vessels, N pyramidal neurons, O nuclei.","w":["Glia too.","Glia, not fibroblasts.","Neurons.","Rarely."],"k":"Nervous tissue cells."},{"id":14089,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"Which statement about innate immunity and autophagy is correct?","o":["It is important in innate immunity against infection","It suppresses innate immunity during infection","It occurs only during starvation","It causes infection by releasing toxins","It is unrelated to immunity"],"e":"Autophagy is important in <b>innate immunity</b> as a response to <b>infection</b>.","w":["Important.","Not only.","Not.","Related."],"k":"Autophagy in innate immunity.","rn":[["","What role does autophagy play in immunity?","What role does autophagy play in immunity?","Innate immunity; Response to infection"]]},{"id":14090,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"What feature distinguishes the autophagosome membrane?","o":["A double membrane around a portion of cytoplasm","A single membrane, identical to the lysosome","A double membrane around the nucleus","A single membrane derived from the cell surface","No membrane at all"],"e":"A portion of the cytoplasm is surrounded by a <b>double membrane</b>, creating an <b>autophagosome</b>.","w":["Double.","Not nuclear.","Endosome.","It has one."],"k":"Autophagosome = double membrane.","rn":[["","Autophagosome","Autophagosome","Cytoplasm portion; In a double membrane"]]},{"id":14091,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"A cell type lines kidney tubules and absorbs substances. Which shape and arrangement?","o":["Simple cuboidal epithelium","Stratified cuboidal epithelium","Simple squamous epithelium","Transitional epithelium","Pseudostratified ciliated"],"e":"<b>Cuboidal</b> epithelium lines <b>kidney tubules</b> and glands (secretion, absorption), in a simple layer.","w":["Simple.","Diffusion.","Bladder.","Airway."],"k":"Kidney tubules = simple cuboidal.","rn":[["","Which epithelium: kidney tubules?","Which epithelium: kidney tubules?","Simple cuboidal"]]},{"id":14092,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which of the following would most likely contain dense irregular connective tissue?","o":["The dermis","A tendon","A ligament","The spleen","Blood"],"e":"<b>Dense irregular</b>: <b>glands, dermis</b> – collagen in multiple directions.","w":["Regular.","Regular.","Reticular.","Fluid."],"k":"Dermis = dense irregular.","rn":[["","Where is dense irregular connective tissue found?","Where is dense irregular connective tissue found?","Glands; Dermis"]]},{"id":14093,"t":"H&E Staining","s":"Which statement summarises the purpose of H&E in histology?","o":["The universal stain: nuclei purple, cytoplasm and fibres pink","The universal stain: nuclei pink, cytoplasm and fibres purple","A special stain for collagen types","A special stain for reticular fibres","A stain that shows organelles in detail"],"e":"H&E is the “universal staining pattern”: <b>purple nucleus</b>, <b>pink cytoplasm and fibres</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Cannot.","Not.","Not."],"k":"H&E: universal stain.","rn":[["","Which stain is used for most histology slides?","Which stain is used for most histology slides?","Haematoxylin and eosin (H&E); A universal staining pattern"],["","How do different cells differ on H&E?","How do different cells differ on H&E?","Morphology (shape); Staining shade; More/less pink or purple"]]},{"id":14094,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"This tissue consists of large empty-looking cells with a thin rim of cytoplasm and a flattened nucleus at the edge. Name the tissue.","e":"Adipose tissue (adipocytes/fat cells): specialised connective tissue for protection, insulation and energy store.","k":"Adipose tissue.","img":{"src":"14/adipose","cap":"Specialised connective tissue, H&amp;E."},"a":["adipose","adipose tissue","fat","white adipose tissue","fat tissue"],"rn":[["","What are the 3 functions of adipose tissue?","What are the 3 functions of adipose tissue?","Protection for internal organs; Insulation; Energy store"],["","Adipose Tissue","Adipose Tissue","Fat tissue"],["","What are the 3 types of specialised connective tissue?","What are the 3 types of specialised connective tissue?","Bone; Adipose; Cartilage"]]},{"id":14095,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"A malignant tumour arises from glandular epithelium. Name it.","e":"Uncontrolled epithelial division gives adenoma (benign) or carcinoma; a malignant glandular tumour is an adenocarcinoma.","k":"Adenocarcinoma.","a":["adenocarcinoma"],"rn":[["","What are 2 examples of epithelial cancers?","What are 2 examples of epithelial cancers?","Carcinomas; Adenocarcinomas"]]},{"id":14096,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which pairing is correct for collagen type III?","o":["Small fibres: vessels, organs, marrow, lymphoid","Large fibres in tendons, skin and bone","Sheet-like layers in basement membranes","Small fibres in cartilage only","Thin fibrils in smooth and skeletal muscle basement membranes"],"e":"<b>Type III</b>: small fibres – blood vessels, parenchymal organs, bone marrow, lymphoid tissues (reticular).","w":["Type I.","Type IV.","Type II.","Type V."],"k":"Type III locations.","rn":[["","Type III collagen: tissue","Type III collagen: tissue","Blood vessels; Parenchymal organs; Bone marrow; Lymphoid tissues"]]},{"id":14097,"t":"Glands","s":"A secretory cell releases its product by exocytosis without losing any membrane. Name the secretion mode.","e":"Merocrine secretion: exocytosis with no loss of membrane.","k":"Merocrine.","a":["merocrine","eccrine"],"rn":[["","Merocrine","Merocrine","Secretes without disrupting; The cell membrane"],["","What are the 3 modes of exocrine secretion?","What are the 3 modes of exocrine secretion?","Merocrine; Apocrine; Holocrine"]]},{"id":14098,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"Name the double-membraned structure labelled <b>A</b>, which encloses cytosol and organelles.","e":"A = autophagosome: a double membrane forms around a portion of cytoplasm and then fuses with a lysosome for degradation.","k":"A = autophagosome.","img":{"src":"14/autophagy","cap":"Pathway of a cellular degradation process. Labels removed."},"a":["autophagosome","autophagosomes","autophagic vacuole"],"rn":[["","Autophagosome","Autophagosome","Cytoplasm portion; In a double membrane"]]},{"id":14100,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"This epithelium lines the small intestine, where it absorbs nutrients. Name it (shape and arrangement).","e":"Simple columnar epithelium: one layer of tall cells with basal nuclei; found in the GI tract and uterine tubes; absorption and secretion. Larger cytoplasm makes it “more functional” than squamous.","k":"Simple columnar.","img":{"src":"14/columnar","cap":"Gut lining, H&amp;E. N = nucleus."},"a":["simple columnar","simple columnar epithelium","columnar"],"rn":[["","Which epithelium: GI tract?","Which epithelium: GI tract?","Simple columnar"]]},{"id":14101,"t":"Glands","s":"Which is the best description of a goblet cell?","o":["A unicellular mucus gland in gut/airway","A multicellular mucus gland in the skin","A unicellular protein-secreting gland in the pancreas","An endocrine cell in the gut","A fibroblast secreting mucus"],"e":"<b>Goblet cell</b>: unicellular mucus-secreting gland.","w":["Unicellular.","Mucus.","Exocrine.","Not."],"k":"Goblet cell definition.","rn":[["","Unicellular Gland","Unicellular Gland","One cell; e.g. goblet cells"]]},{"id":14102,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which statement about type V collagen is correct?","o":["Thin fibrils in muscle basement membranes","Large fibres in tendons","Small fibres in cartilage","Reticular fibres in lymph nodes","Sheet-like layers in all basement membranes"],"e":"<b>Type V</b>: thin fibrils, basement membranes (smooth, skeletal muscle).","w":["Type I.","Type II.","Type III.","Type IV."],"k":"Type V = thin fibrils.","rn":[["","Type V collagen: tissue","Type V collagen: tissue","Basement membranes (smooth, skeletal muscle)"],["","Type V collagen: morphology","Type V collagen: morphology","Thin fibrils"]]},{"id":14103,"t":"Glands","s":"Which explanation best links glands to epithelium?","o":["Glands are built from epithelial cells","Glands are built from connective tissue cells","Glands are built from smooth muscle cells","Glands are built from nervous tissue","Glands are built from fibroblasts and collagen"],"e":"Glands are “constructed” from <b>epithelial cells</b>, including specially adapted <b>secretory</b> epithelial cells.","w":["Epithelium.","Myoepithelial cells help expel.","Not.","Epithelium."],"k":"Glands are epithelial.","rn":[["","What are glands constructed from?","What are glands constructed from?","Epithelial cells; Including secretory epithelial cells"]]},{"id":14104,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which statement about elastic fibres in loose connective tissue is correct?","o":["Some elastic fibres, sparse type I collagen","Only elastic fibres, with no collagen","No elastic fibres, only dense collagen","Elastic fibres made of type IV collagen","Elastic fibres only in cartilage"],"e":"Loose CT: sparse, randomly arranged type I collagen and <b>some elastic fibres</b>.","w":["Collagen too.","Sparse.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Loose CT has some elastic fibres.","rn":[["","Loose connective tissue","Loose connective tissue","Many cells; Sparse, random type I collagen; Some elastic fibres"],["","What are the 2 types of fibre in loose connective tissue?","What are the 2 types of fibre in loose connective tissue?","Collagen fibres; Elastic fibres"]]},{"id":14105,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"This epithelium lines kidney tubules. Which type is it, and what is its main function?","o":["Simple cuboidal; secretion and absorption","Simple squamous; diffusion and filtration","Stratified cuboidal; protection","Simple columnar; absorption with microvilli only","Transitional; stretching"],"e":"Simple cuboidal epithelium (glands, kidney tubules): one layer of cube-like cells with round central nuclei; secretion and absorption.","w":["Squamous cells are flat with flattened nuclei.","There is only one layer of cells here.","The cells are as tall as they are wide, not tall columns.","Transitional epithelium has several layers."],"k":"Kidney tubule = simple cuboidal.","img":{"src":"14/cuboidal","cap":"Tubule lining, H&amp;E. N = nucleus."},"rn":[["","What is the function of cuboidal epithelium?","What is the function of cuboidal epithelium?","Secretion; Absorption"],["","Which epithelium: kidney tubules?","Which epithelium: kidney tubules?","Simple cuboidal"]]},{"id":14106,"t":"Glands","s":"A gut biopsy shows clear, pale-staining cells scattered among columnar cells. What are they most likely?","o":["Goblet cells containing mucus","Serous cells containing protein","Fibroblasts making collagen","Adipocytes containing fat","Myoepithelial cells"],"e":"<b>Mucus</b> looks <b>clear</b> on H&E; goblet cells (unicellular glands) contain mucus.","w":["Pink.","Not.","Not stated.","Not."],"k":"Clear cells = mucus/goblet.","rn":[["","What do goblet cells produce?","What do goblet cells produce?","Mucus"]]},{"id":14107,"t":"Glands","s":"Name the secretion mode shown in <b>C</b>, which needs the highest rate of cell renewal.","e":"C = holocrine: the whole cell disintegrates to release its product.","k":"C = holocrine.","img":{"src":"14/secretion","cap":"Three modes of exocrine secretion. Names removed."},"a":["holocrine"],"rn":[["","Holocrine","Holocrine","Whole cell disrupted; To release contents"],["","Why does holocrine secretion need high turnover?","Why does holocrine secretion need high turnover?","Whole cell is disrupted; Needs renewal"],["","What are the 3 modes of exocrine secretion?","What are the 3 modes of exocrine secretion?","Merocrine; Apocrine; Holocrine"]]},{"id":14108,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Which statement about lung epithelium is correct?","o":["Has stem cells; squamous cells aid diffusion","Lung epithelium is keratinised to resist damage","Lung epithelium is transitional, to allow stretching","Lung epithelium has no stem or progenitor cells","Lung epithelium is mainly goblet cells"],"e":"Squamous epithelium is found in the <b>lungs</b> (diffusion, filtration), and <b>lung epithelium</b> has stem/progenitor cells for renewal.","w":["Not.","Not.","It has.","Not."],"k":"Lung epithelium: squamous, renews.","rn":[["","What is the function of squamous epithelium?","What is the function of squamous epithelium?","Diffusion; Filtration; Secretion"],["","What are the 3 examples of epithelial stem/progenitor cells?","What are the 3 examples of epithelial stem/progenitor cells?","Intestinal stem cells; Hair follicle cells; Lung epithelium"]]},{"id":14109,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"Name the organelle labelled <b>B</b>, containing acid hydrolases, which fuses with A.","e":"B = lysosome: acid hydrolases break down proteins, nucleic acids, lipids and sugars at low pH.","k":"B = lysosome.","img":{"src":"14/autophagy","cap":"Pathway of a cellular degradation process. Labels removed."},"a":["lysosome","lysosomes"],"rn":[["","Is an autophagosome a lysosome?","Is an autophagosome a lysosome?","No; It fuses with a lysosome"],["","Lysosome","Lysosome","Organelle with digestive enzymes; Low pH"]]},{"id":14110,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which statement about reticular tissue's appearance in later teaching is correct?","o":["In immunology, as lymph node framework","It returns in bone teaching as compact bone","It returns in skin teaching as the dermis","It is not taught again","It returns as tendon tissue"],"e":"Reticular tissue comes up again in <b>immunology</b> – the connective tissue of lymph nodes.","w":["Not.","Dense irregular.","Not.","Dense regular."],"k":"Reticular CT in immunology.","rn":[["","Where is reticular connective tissue found?","Where is reticular connective tissue found?","Spleen; Lymph nodes; Liver"]]},{"id":14111,"t":"Muscle Tissue","s":"Which tissue combination would you expect in a cross-section of a hollow internal organ from inside out?","o":["Epithelium, then smooth muscle","Smooth muscle, then epithelium","Epithelium, then skeletal muscle","Connective tissue, then epithelium","Keratin, then smooth muscle"],"e":"From inside an organ outwards: <b>epithelium</b> first, then <b>smooth muscle</b> as the outer layer.","w":["Reversed.","Smooth.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Epithelium inside, smooth muscle outside.","rn":[["","Where does smooth muscle sit in an organ (inside out)?","Where does smooth muscle sit in an organ (inside out)?","After the epithelium"]]},{"id":14112,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Which statement about epithelium being “widespread” is correct?","o":["Found throughout the body","Found only in skin and glands","Found only lining hollow organs","Absent from blood vessels","A form of connective tissue"],"e":"Epithelium is <b>widespread</b>, distinguished by <b>shape</b> (squamous, cuboidal, columnar) and <b>arrangement</b> (simple, stratified, pseudostratified, transitional).","w":["Widespread.","Widespread.","Endothelium.","Not."],"k":"Epithelium is widespread."},{"id":14113,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"Which pairing of lysosomal disorder and feature is correct?","o":["Storage diseases – genetic, undigested molecules","I-cell disease – too many hydrolases inside lysosomes","Early-onset Parkinson’s – excess collagen","Tau accumulation – a skin disease","Coeliac disease – a lysosomal disorder"],"e":"<b>Lysosomal storage diseases</b> lead to accumulation of <b>undigested biomolecules</b> with a <b>genetic cause</b>.","w":["Secreted instead.","Mitophagy.","Neuro.","Autoimmune."],"k":"Storage diseases: genetic.","rn":[["","What is the cause of lysosomal storage diseases?","What is the cause of lysosomal storage diseases?","Genetic"],["","What do lysosomal storage diseases cause?","What do lysosomal storage diseases cause?","Accumulation of undigested biomolecules"],["","What causes lysosomal storage diseases?","What causes lysosomal storage diseases?","Genetic causes"]]},{"id":14114,"t":"Glands","s":"Which tissue would you examine to see cells arranged in acini producing protein?","o":["A serous exocrine gland","A mucous exocrine gland","A tendon","Transitional epithelium","Cerebral cortex"],"e":"Protein-secreting cells arranged in <b>acini</b> are found in <b>serous</b> glands (protein secretion).","w":["Mucus.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Serous acini.","rn":[["","What do serous glands secrete?","What do serous glands secrete?","Protein"],["","What are the 3 types of gland by secretion?","What are the 3 types of gland by secretion?","Serous; Mucous; Sebaceous"]]},{"id":14115,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"Which statement about why connective tissue self-renewal matters is given?","o":["Otherwise we could never heal a cut","Otherwise we could never grow taller","It prevents all forms of disease","It stops tissue ageing","It produces new nerve cells"],"e":"Connective tissue self-renews, “<b>otherwise we would never be able to heal from a cut</b>”.","w":["Not stated.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"CT renewal enables healing."},{"id":14116,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Which explanation best accounts for why squamous cells suit diffusion?","o":["They are flat and thin, a short barrier","They have large cytoplasm for transport","They are multilayered for strength","They have cilia to move gases","They are keratinised to stay dry"],"e":"<b>Squamous</b> cells are flat and compressed – barrier tissues involved in <b>diffusion, filtration</b> (e.g. lungs, vessels).","w":["Cuboidal.","Not.","Not.","Not."],"k":"Flat squamous → diffusion."},{"id":14117,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"This single layer of flat cells with flattened nuclei lines the inside of a capillary. What is the specific name for this lining?","e":"Flat (squamous-like) cells lining vessels are called endothelium, named for their function and origin; those lining membranes are mesothelium. Squamous cells suit diffusion and filtration: a short barrier.","k":"Endothelium.","img":{"src":"14/squamous","cap":"Lining surface, H&amp;E. N = nucleus."},"a":["endothelium","endothelial","endothelial cells","vascular endothelium"],"rn":[["","Endothelium","Endothelium","Squamous epithelium lining blood vessels"],["","What is squamous epithelium called in vessels and membranes?","What is squamous epithelium called in vessels and membranes?","Vessels: endothelium; Membranes: mesothelium"]]},{"id":14118,"t":"Glands","s":"Which labelling of secretion modes A, B and C is correct?","o":["A merocrine; B apocrine; C holocrine","A apocrine; B merocrine; C holocrine","A holocrine; B apocrine; C merocrine","A merocrine; B holocrine; C apocrine","A apocrine; B holocrine; C merocrine"],"e":"Merocrine: exocytosis, no membrane lost. Apocrine: vesicles take part of the cell membrane with them. Holocrine: the whole cell is disrupted, so high cell turnover is needed (e.g. sebaceous glands).","w":["A releases vesicles by exocytosis with no membrane loss: merocrine.","C, the whole cell disintegrating, is holocrine.","B pinches off a portion of the cell with membrane: apocrine.","B loses only part of the cell: apocrine."],"k":"Mero, apo, holo = none, part, whole.","img":{"src":"14/secretion","cap":"Three modes of exocrine secretion. Names removed."},"rn":[["","What are the 3 modes of exocrine secretion?","What are the 3 modes of exocrine secretion?","Merocrine; Apocrine; Holocrine"]]},{"id":14119,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"Which statement about apoptosis's relation to lysosomes is correct?","o":["It uses neither lysosomes nor autophagosomes","It requires lysosome fusion to proceed","It is a form of autophagy","It is reversible once started","It forms endolysosomes"],"e":"<b>Apoptosis</b>: digestive enzymes act <b>in the cytoplasm</b>; <b>no lysosomes or autophagosomes</b>; irreversible.","w":["Not.","Different.","Irreversible.","Endocytosis."],"k":"Apoptosis: no lysosomes.","rn":[["","Autophagy vs apoptosis: machinery","Autophagy vs apoptosis: machinery","Autophagy: lysosomes and autophagosomes; Apoptosis: enzymes in cytoplasm"]]},{"id":14120,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"Which combination best summarises epithelium?","o":["Three shapes, four arrangements, turnover, cancer risk","Three shapes, one arrangement, low turnover","One shape, four arrangements, high turnover","Three shapes, four arrangements, no turnover","Three shapes, four arrangements, and no disease links"],"e":"Epithelium: <b>three shapes</b> × arrangements (simple, stratified, pseudostratified, transitional); <b>high turnover</b> via stem/progenitor cells; risks of adenoma/carcinoma and damage outpacing renewal (coeliac).","w":["Four arrangements; high turnover.","Three shapes.","Turnover.","Disease."],"k":"Epithelium summary."},{"id":14121,"t":"H&E Staining","s":"A pathology student stains a section of liver with haematoxylin and eosin. The nuclei stain deep purple and the cytoplasm stains pink. Her tutor asks her to explain why the nucleus takes up the purple dye in terms of the chemistry of the dye and the structures.<br><br>Which explanation is correct?","o":["Haematoxylin is basic and stains acidic DNA/RNA purple; eosin is acidic and stains basic proteins pink","Haematoxylin is acidic and stains basic proteins purple; eosin stains DNA pink","Both dyes are basic, so they stain everything the same","Eosin stains nuclei because DNA is a basic structure","Haematoxylin is basic and stains the cytoplasmic proteins and extracellular fibres purple, while eosin stains nuclei"],"e":"Step 1: haematoxylin is a basic dye, so it stains acidic (basophilic) structures purple, such as DNA in the nucleus and nucleolus, RNA and cartilage carbohydrates. Step 2: eosin is an acidic dye, so it stains basic (acidophilic) structures pink, such as cytoplasmic proteins and extracellular fibres.","w":["Haematoxylin is a basic dye that stains acidic structures.","The two dyes are opposite: one basic, one acidic.","DNA is acidic and is stained by haematoxylin.","Haematoxylin stains acidic structures such as DNA; eosin stains cytoplasm."],"k":"Haematoxylin (basic) → acidic DNA purple; eosin (acidic) → basic proteins pink.","rn":[["","Haematoxylin","Haematoxylin","Basic dye; Stains acidic (basophilic) structures; Purple"],["","Eosin","Eosin","Acidic dye; Stains basic (acidophilic) structures; Pink"]]},{"id":14122,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"A pathologist examines three biopsies. Biopsy A, from the trachea, has a single layer of cells with cilia, all touching the basement membrane but with nuclei at different heights. Biopsy B, from the bladder, has a variable number of layers. Biopsy C, from the skin, has many layers with a surface of keratin.<br><br>Which types of epithelium are these, and what is the function of B?","o":["A: pseudostratified ciliated; B: transitional (allows stretching); C: keratinised stratified squamous","A: stratified columnar; B: simple cuboidal (secretion); C: non-keratinised stratified squamous","A: simple squamous; B: transitional (diffusion); C: pseudostratified","A: pseudostratified ciliated; B: stratified squamous, which protects the bladder against injury from urine; C: transitional","A: transitional; B: pseudostratified; C: simple columnar"],"e":"Step 1: a single layer that looks layered, with nuclei at different heights and cilia, is pseudostratified ciliated epithelium (trachea, propels mucus); keratinised stratified squamous epithelium is skin. Step 2: transitional epithelium has a variable number of layers, lines the bladder and urethra, and allows stretching of the lining.","w":["All cells touching the basement membrane means pseudostratified, not stratified.","Simple squamous is a single flat layer, and transitional allows stretching.","The bladder lining is transitional, not stratified squamous.","The trachea is pseudostratified and skin is stratified squamous."],"k":"Trachea: pseudostratified ciliated; bladder: transitional; skin: keratinised stratified squamous.","rn":[["","What is the function of transitional epithelium?","What is the function of transitional epithelium?","Allows stretching of lining"],["","Where is keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Where is keratinised stratified squamous epithelium?","Skin"],["","What are the 4 epithelial organisations?","What are the 4 epithelial organisations?","Simple; Stratified; Pseudostratified; Transitional"]]},{"id":14123,"t":"Epithelial Tissue","s":"A 30-year-old woman with coeliac disease has an autoimmune response that damages the lining of her small intestine. Her doctor explains that this lining normally renews itself rapidly from stem cells, but in her case the damage is happening too quickly. A separate patient has a benign glandular tumour of the colon.<br><br>Which statement best explains both conditions?","o":["Coeliac: damage outpaces renewal; the tumour is an adenoma, a risk of high turnover","Coeliac: renewal stops entirely; the tumour is a carcinoma","Coeliac: the epithelium has no stem cells; the tumour is an adenocarcinoma","Coeliac: autophagy fails; the tumour is from muscle tissue","Coeliac: lysosomal storage disease; the tumour is a lymphoma"],"e":"Step 1: epithelial tissue has high cell turnover, with renewal from tissue-specific stem cells (e.g. Lgr5 intestinal stem cells); in coeliac disease damage from an autoimmune response is faster than renewal, impairing organ function. Step 2: the ability to renew carries a risk: benign tumours (adenomas) and malignant transformation (carcinomas, adenocarcinomas); a benign glandular tumour is an adenoma.","w":["Renewal continues but is outpaced, and a benign tumour is an adenoma.","Intestinal epithelium has stem cells (Lgr5).","Coeliac damage is autoimmune, and an adenoma is epithelial.","Coeliac disease is not a lysosomal storage disease."],"k":"Coeliac: damage > renewal; high turnover → adenoma/carcinoma risk.","rn":[["","What are the 3 types of epithelium disease?","What are the 3 types of epithelium disease?","Benign tumours (adenomas); Malignant transformation (cancers); Damage faster than renewal"],["","What is the example of damage faster than renewal?","What is the example of damage faster than renewal?","Coeliac disease; Due to an autoimmune response"]]},{"id":14124,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"A research lab studies four patients with different genetic collagen defects. Patient 1 has fragile tendons and skin. Patient 2 has abnormal cartilage. Patient 3 has abnormal basement membranes in the kidneys. Patient 4 has weak blood vessels and abnormal lymphoid tissue.<br><br>Which collagen type is most likely affected in each patient?","o":["1: type I; 2: type II; 3: type IV; 4: type III","1: type II; 2: type I; 3: type III; 4: type IV","1: type III; 2: type IV; 3: type I; 4: type II","1: type IV; 2: type III; 3: type II; 4: type I","All four: type I, because it is the only collagen type found in tendon, cartilage, vessels and basement membranes"],"e":"Step 1: type I collagen forms large fibres in skin, tendon, bone and ligaments, and type II forms small fibres in cartilage. Step 2: type III forms small fibres in blood vessels, parenchymal organs, bone marrow and lymphoid tissues, and type IV forms a sheet-like layer in basement membranes.","w":["Type II is cartilage and type I is tendon/skin.","Type III is vessels/lymphoid, not tendon.","Type IV is basement membranes, not tendon.","Different tissues use different collagen types."],"k":"I: skin/tendon/bone; II: cartilage; III: vessels/lymphoid; IV: basement membrane.","rn":[["","Which tissues have collagen types I to IV?","Which tissues have collagen types I to IV?","I: skin/bone, II: cartilage; III: vessels; IV: basement membrane; MNEMONIC"],["","Type III collagen: tissue","Type III collagen: tissue","Blood vessels; Parenchymal organs; Bone marrow; Lymphoid tissues"]]},{"id":14125,"t":"Connective Tissue","s":"A surgeon repairs a torn ligament and notes that its fibres all run in the same direction. The pathologist then examines tissue from the dermis, where the collagen fibres are tightly packed but run in many directions. Tissue beneath the skin around blood vessels has many cells and sparse, randomly arranged fibres.<br><br>Which types of connective tissue are these?","o":["Ligament: dense regular; dermis: dense irregular; around vessels: loose","Ligament: loose; dermis: dense regular; around vessels: reticular","Ligament: dense irregular; dermis: loose; around vessels: dense regular","Ligament: reticular; dermis: elastic; around vessels: adipose","Ligament: dense regular; dermis: reticular (type III fibres, invisible in H&E); around vessels: dense irregular"],"e":"Step 1: dense regular connective tissue has tightly packed type I collagen fibres in the same orientation (ligaments, tendons), and dense irregular has tightly packed type I fibres in multiple directions (glands, dermis). Step 2: loose connective tissue, beneath the skin and around vessels, muscles and nerves, has many cells with sparse, randomly arranged collagen and some elastic fibres.","w":["Ligaments are dense regular, not loose.","Ligament fibres run in one direction, so they are regular.","Reticular tissue supports soft organs like the spleen.","The dermis is dense irregular, and tissue around vessels is loose."],"k":"Dense regular (ligament), dense irregular (dermis), loose (around vessels).","rn":[["","Where is loose connective tissue found?","Where is loose connective tissue found?","Beneath the skin; Around blood vessels; Around muscles and nerves"],["","Where is dense irregular connective tissue found?","Where is dense irregular connective tissue found?","Glands; Dermis"],["","What are the 3 types of dense connective tissue?","What are the 3 types of dense connective tissue?","Regular; Irregular; Elastic"]]},{"id":14126,"t":"Glands","s":"A histology student studies three glands. In gland X, the cells release their product without any damage to the cell membrane. In gland Y, the product leaves in vesicles that take part of the membrane with them. In gland Z, whole cells break apart to release their contents, so the gland needs a high rate of cell renewal.<br><br>Which modes of secretion are these?","o":["X: merocrine; Y: apocrine; Z: holocrine","X: holocrine; Y: merocrine; Z: apocrine","X: apocrine; Y: holocrine; Z: merocrine","X: endocrine; Y: exocrine; Z: serous","X: merocrine; Y: holocrine; Z: apocrine, because the vesicles damage the whole cell membrane"],"e":"Step 1: merocrine glands secrete without disruption of the cell membrane, while apocrine glands secrete in vesicles that take part of the membrane. Step 2: holocrine glands disrupt the whole cell to release its contents, so the cell is damaged and high turnover and renewal are needed.","w":["Holocrine destroys the whole cell, which fits Z, not X.","Apocrine takes part of the membrane, which fits Y.","Endocrine/exocrine describe where secretion goes, not the mode.","Whole-cell disruption is holocrine, which fits Z."],"k":"Merocrine: no damage; apocrine: part membrane; holocrine: whole cell.","rn":[["","What are the 3 modes of exocrine secretion?","What are the 3 modes of exocrine secretion?","Merocrine; Apocrine; Holocrine"]]},{"id":14127,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"A cell biologist starves a culture of cells. The cells form double-membrane vesicles around parts of their cytoplasm, which then fuse with lysosomes and are digested, and most cells survive. In a second culture exposed to a toxin, the cells begin a programme of death in which digestive enzymes act in the cytoplasm without lysosomes, and they cannot recover.<br><br>Which processes are these?","o":["Culture 1: autophagy; culture 2: apoptosis","Culture 1: apoptosis; culture 2: autophagy","Both are autophagy, as both digest cell contents","Culture 1: holocrine secretion; culture 2: mitophagy","Both are apoptosis, because the cells in culture 1 also use lysosomes to die"],"e":"Step 1: autophagy (\"self-eating\") encircles cytoplasm in an autophagosome, which fuses with a lysosome for digestion; it can be non-selective in starvation, is a maintenance process, can be reversible and the cell usually survives. Step 2: apoptosis is programmed cell death in which digestive enzymes act in the cytoplasm with no lysosomes or autophagosomes, and there is no going back once initiated.","w":["The two processes are swapped.","The second culture uses no lysosomes and dies, which is apoptosis.","Holocrine secretion is a gland mode, and mitophagy is removal of mitochondria.","Culture 1 cells survive, which fits autophagy."],"k":"Autophagy: autophagosome + lysosome, cell survives; apoptosis: death, no lysosomes.","rn":[["","Autophagy vs apoptosis: machinery","Autophagy vs apoptosis: machinery","Autophagy: lysosomes and autophagosomes; Apoptosis: enzymes in cytoplasm"],["","Autophagy vs apoptosis: outcome","Autophagy vs apoptosis: outcome","Autophagy: cell can survive; Apoptosis: cell dies"]]},{"id":14128,"t":"Autophagy and Lysosomes","s":"A 45-year-old man develops early-onset Parkinson's disease, and his genetic test shows a defect in the process that removes damaged mitochondria. His elderly aunt has a neurodegenerative disorder in which tau proteins accumulate in her neurons.<br><br>Which mechanisms are most likely involved in each?","o":["Man: impaired mitophagy; aunt: declining autophagic removal of tau","Man: excess apoptosis of neurons; aunt: excess autophagy","Man: impaired holocrine secretion; aunt: impaired collagen production","Man: increased lysosomal pH; aunt: excess mitophagy","Man: a lysosomal storage disease of the skin; aunt: normal ageing, as autophagy increases with age"],"e":"Step 1: damaged mitochondria are removed through mitophagy, and impaired mitophagy is linked to a form of early-onset Parkinson's disease. Step 2: impaired autophagy can cause accumulation of molecules, and in neurodegenerative disorders the accumulation of tau has been attributed to a decline in its autophagic removal; autophagy also declines with ageing.","w":["The lecture links these to impaired, not excess, removal.","Holocrine secretion and collagen are not involved.","Tau accumulation is from declining autophagy, not excess mitophagy.","Autophagy gradually declines, not increases, with age."],"k":"Impaired mitophagy → early Parkinson's; ↓ autophagy → tau build-up.","rn":[["","Why do tau proteins accumulate?","Why do tau proteins accumulate?","Decline in autophagic removal"],["","Impaired mitophagy is linked to what?","Impaired mitophagy is linked to what?","Early onset Parkinson's"]]},{"id":14129,"t":"Muscle Tissue","s":"A medical student examines two muscle samples. Sample A, from the abdominal wall, has long parallel cells with many nuclei and a striped appearance. Sample B, from the wall of the uterus, has no stripes and elongated nuclei, and it forms the outer layers of the organ.<br><br>Which muscle types are these, and why does A look striped?","o":["A: skeletal, striped from actin and myosin filaments; B: smooth","A: smooth, striped from collagen fibres; B: skeletal","A: cardiac, striped from intercalated discs; B: smooth","A: skeletal, striped from keratin; B: cardiac","A: smooth, striped from its many nuclei; B: skeletal"],"e":"Step 1: skeletal muscle (limbs, abdominal and thoracic walls) has parallel muscle fibres with multiple nuclei and a striated appearance due to actin and myosin filaments. Step 2: smooth muscle (internal organs such as the GI tract, vessels and uterus) has no striation and elongated nuclei.","w":["Smooth muscle has no striations.","The abdominal wall is skeletal muscle, not cardiac.","Striations are from actin and myosin, not keratin.","Striations come from actin and myosin, not nuclei."],"k":"Skeletal: striated (actin/myosin), multinucleate; smooth: no stripes, elongated nuclei.","rn":[["","Why is skeletal muscle striated?","Why is skeletal muscle striated?","Actin and myosin filaments"]]},{"id":14130,"t":"Glands","s":"A biopsy of the small intestine shows single tall cells scattered among the absorptive lining cells, each filled with a pale secretion released onto the surface. A separate biopsy of a salivary gland shows many cells grouped into acini that release secretions through a duct.<br><br>Which gland types are these, and what tissue are glands built from?","o":["Goblet cells (unicellular) and a multicellular exocrine gland; both from epithelium","Endocrine cells and a ductless gland; both from connective tissue","Goblet cells and an endocrine gland; both from muscle tissue","Holocrine cells and a serous gland; both from nervous tissue","Two endocrine glands, because both release secretions directly into the blood rather than through a duct"],"e":"Step 1: glands are constructed from epithelial cells, including specially adapted secretory epithelial cells. Step 2: they can be unicellular, like goblet cells in intestinal and respiratory epithelium, or multicellular, secreting through a duct onto an epithelial surface, as in salivary glands (exocrine).","w":["Glands are built from epithelium, and the salivary gland has a duct.","The salivary gland has a duct, so it is exocrine, and glands are epithelial.","Glands are epithelial, not nervous tissue.","Both release onto a surface; endocrine glands are ductless and release into blood."],"k":"Glands are epithelial: unicellular (goblet) or multicellular via ducts.","rn":[["","Unicellular Gland","Unicellular Gland","One cell; e.g. goblet cells"],["","What are the 2 types of gland by cell number?","What are the 2 types of gland by cell number?","Unicellular; Multicellular"],["","Multicellular gland","Multicellular gland","Many cells; Secrete via a duct; Onto epithelial surface"]]}]);
